From: =?UTF-8?q?=E4=BB=93=E9=BC=A0=E7=8E=8B?= <yjianbin419@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 22:42:11 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] feat(ord0253): claim the key in its own committed
 transaction before any side effect runs

Phase A inserts the NULL claim and commits it on a connection of its own, so the
unique index adjudicates ownership before impl can write anything; the loser is
answered by reading the winner, never by entering impl. Phase B takes commit
ownership away from the callee: the three session.commit() calls inside
_run_agent_command_impl only flush, and the placeholder is transitioned
NULL -> completed receipt inside the single real commit, so a failure between
them leaves no durable mission/run/audit/event and keeps the claim as evidence.

The previous version relied on join_transaction_mode=create_savepoint to keep
those inner commits inside an outer transaction. Measured false: pysqlite does
not emit BEGIN before a SAVEPOINT, so releasing the outermost savepoint commits,
and the loser's mission row was already durable when the key conflicted. The
five mechanical gates from the ruling are now in the door, judged on rows and on
statement/commit order read off the engine.
